dcomp: draw a tree with one visual straight onto the window; process check; quieter

Follow-up to the four Cubase 14 patches before it (off-screen composition,
compose thread, alpha blend, recompose on restore), for what they cost
programs that do not need them.

1. The off-screen frame keeps a partly composed frame off the screen and puts
   a child target over its parent's frame. A tree with one drawing visual and
   no composed window above it has neither, and paid a window-sized copy more
   every tick for it. A JUCE 8 plugin editor is exactly that (one swapchain
   visual). Such a tree is drawn straight onto the window, as before the
   series. The count follows shared visuals; the look for a composed ancestor
   is skipped when the process has a single composed window.

2. wined3d set or removed the __wine_dcomp_comp_alpha window property on every
   present. A property is a server call; an editor that presents as fast as it
   can paid for it on every frame. It is now set when the composition buffer
   is created.

3. The composed-window property holds the target pointer. An ancestor owned by
   another process would hand over that process's pointer: the walk stops at
   the first window of another process.

4. "Target has no root" printed a FIXME every tick now that the thread keeps
   running; it is a TRACE.

Measured in REAPER with the editor open and parameters moving, percent of one
core (stock / series as submitted / with this patch):
  SINE Player  60 / 71-72 / 60-61
  Squash       121 / 124-126 / 119-122
  Kontakt 8    16 / 16 / 16
The off-screen path itself (several visuals, child targets) is unchanged and
was not exercised here: no Cubase on the test machine.

